{
  "abstract": "Let $\\phi_j(x)$ be the parity of the $j$-th Collatz iterate. Let $\\Sigma_j$ be the set of residues modulo $2^{j+1}$ whose coefficient stopping time exceeds $j$, that is, $3^{s_t}>2^t$ for $1\\le t\\le j$, where $s_t$ counts the odd steps among the first $t$. We study the normalised coefficients\n$$g_j(c,r)=\\frac1{|\\Sigma_j|}\\sum_{x\\in\\Sigma_j}\\phi_j(x)\\,e\\!\\left(-\\frac{c\\,3^{k_j}x}{2^{j+1}}\\right),\\qquad k_j=\\lfloor\\alpha(j+1)\\rfloor-2+r,\\quad \\alpha=\\log_32,$$\nfor fixed odd $c$ with $3\\nmid c$. On $\\Sigma_j$ an odd step at time $i$ contributes the phase $c\\,3^{r-3-\\theta_j}3^{-x_i}$, where $x_i=s_i-\\alpha i>0$ and $\\theta_j=\\{\\alpha(j+1)\\}$; only the last $s_j-k_j$ odd steps are exceptions. The coefficient is carried by the ends of the parity word, where the walk $x$ is near $0$. We prove that $g_j(c,r)\\to S(c,r,\\theta)\\,E(c,r,\\theta)$ along every sequence with $\\theta_j\\to\\theta\\notin(\\mathbb Z+\\alpha\\mathbb Z)$. $S$ is the characteristic function of an explicit functional of the walk conditioned to stay positive, and $E$ is an average over end words. The proof uses the tilt and killed-walk bounds of Paper 87, the invariance principle of [3], a uniform $O(T^{-1/2})$ bound for the middle phases, and decoupling of the ends. We show $S\\ne0$ when $2\\pi|c|3^{r-3-\\theta}\\,\\mathbb E^+Y<1$, with $\\mathbb E^+Y\\le220$. Since every error beyond the end window carries a factor $3^r$, explicit renewal bounds certify $E\\ne0$ at $c=1$, $r\\in\\{-10,-12,-15,-20\\}$, for every such $\\theta$ (computer-assisted, in ball arithmetic), so the limit is nonzero. At $r=0$ this remains open; $E$ vanishes at every odd frequency only if a limit law of the end words on the $3$-adic solenoid is invariant under its element of order $2$. Double-precision computations at $c=1$, $r=0$ (not certified) give $|g_j|\\approx0.04$ for $800\\le j\\le2060$. The same argument gives the limit of Paper 87's unsigned survivor coefficients at the boundary of its decay window; at $r=-20$ it is certified nonzero, so Paper 87's uniform decay fails at bounded $m-\\log_2b$. The result describes the non-stopping set, not individual orbits.",
